nd confidently.\n\nGuidelines:\n\n• Prioritize grammar explanations over vocabulary or general conversation.\n• Break complex grammar into simple, logical steps.\n• Compare similar grammar structures whenever it improves understanding.\n• Explain not only what is correct, but also why.\n• Use short, natural examples instead of artificial textbook sentences.\n• Highlight common learner mistakes when they are relevant.\n• When appropriate, finish with one short practice exercise that allows the student to immediately apply the grammar point.\n• Adapt the depth of explanation to the student\'s proficiency level.\n• Avoid overwhelming the student with exceptions unless they are necessary.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Explain the difference between "I have lived here for five years" and "I lived here for five years". Give clear explanations and five natural examples'}]"}
{"ts":"2026-08-01 15:30:49,670","level":"INFO","logger":"ai_service","request_id":"0002890ca136","user_id":"-","msg":"Raw resulf from AI: {'id': '37f7c950-31aa-4aff-9ec4-001d85b1374a', 'object': 'chat.completion', 'created': 1785598242, 'model': 'deepseek-v4-flash', 'choices': [{'index': 0, 'message': {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'The main difference is whether the time period is still continuing or already finished.\n\n"I have lived here for five years" means you started living here five years ago and you still live here now. The clock is still running. You are describing a situation that connects the past to the present.\n\n"I lived here for five years" means the five-year period is over. You no longer live there. The clock stopped. You are simply talking about a finished period in your past.\n\nThat is why "for five years" works with both. The tense tells us if the five years includes now or not.\n\nHere are five natural examples:\n\n1. I have lived in London for five years. (And I still live in London now.)\n\n2. I have worked at this company for five years. (I still work there today.)\n\n3. I lived in London for five years before moving to Berlin. (That period is finished.)\n\n4. She has been married to Tom for five years. (She is still married to him now.)\n\n5. We lived in that apartment for five years, then we bought a house. (The stay in the apartment ended.)\n\nA quick tip: if the sentence has a finished time expression like "in 2018" or "last year", you cannot use the present perfect. But "for five years" is neutral, so the tense decides the meaning.\n\nWould you like to try a short exercise? I can give you two sentences and you choose the correct tense.', 'reasoning_content': 'We need to respond in English, as user wrote in English. Need to explain difference between present perfect and past simple with "for five years". Provide clear explanations and five natural examples. Need to follow formatting rules: plain text, short paragraphs, no markdown headings, no bold/italic, use quotes for emphasis. Must be mobile-friendly, clean. Also Grammar Lab mode: prioritize grammar explanations, compare structures, why, common mistakes, maybe practice exercise. But user explicitly asks for explanations and five examples. We\'ll do that.\n\nLet\'s structure: first briefly distinguish: present perfect connects past to now, implies still living there; past simple means finished period, no longer lives there. Then explain with time frame. Then give five natural examples (maybe contrast pairs? User asks five examples, so we can give five examples, probably some of each or contrasting. Better to give examples that illustrate both, perhaps three present perfect and two past simple, or pairs. Let\'s produce clear examples.\n\nCommon mistake: using present perfect with a finished time period. But "for five years" can be used with both, depending on whether period continues.\n\nWe\'ll write friendly, tutor-style.\n\nNeed to ensure no markdown. Also maybe finish with a short practice suggestion. But user specifically asked for explanation and examples. We can add a small question at end.\n\nLet\'s craft.'}, 'logprobs': None, 'finish_reason': 'stop'}], 'usage': {'prompt_tokens': 912, 'completion_tokens': 575, 'total_tokens': 1487, 'prompt_tokens_details': {'cached_tokens': 640}, 'completion_tokens_details': {'reasoning_tokens': 275}, 'prompt_cache_hit_tokens': 640, 'prompt_cache_miss_tokens': 272}, 'system_fingerprint': 'fp_a18b46594c_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402'}"}
