{"ts":"2026-08-01 15:33:49,397","level":"INFO","logger":"ai_service","request_id":"b1a02f5f22e3","user_id":"-","msg":"Raw resulf from AI: {'id': '3a3b3fd9-4540-44d1-95ee-eb1e54acddd5', 'object': 'chat.completion', 'created': 1785598421, 'model': 'deepseek-v4-flash', 'choices': [{'index': 0, 'message': {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Good question. This is one of those rules that even native speakers get wrong, so it\'s worth understanding properly.\n\nThe key is the "subjunctive mood." Normally we say "I was," "he was," "she was." But in unreal or hypothetical situations, English uses a special form called the past subjunctive, and for "be" that form is always "were," no matter what the subject is.\n\nSo "If I were you" means "if I were in your position, which I am not." The "were" signals that this is imaginary, not real. That\'s why it feels more correct in formal English.\n\nWhy does English use a past tense here at all? Because the past feels more distant from reality. Compare "If I am you" (sounds strange, almost impossible) with "If I were you" (clearly hypothetical). The past tense pushes the situation away from the present reality.\n\nNow, about "was": in everyday spoken English, many people say "If I was you," and in informal contexts that is widely accepted. But in formal writing, exams, and fixed expressions like "as it were," the subjunctive "were" is the expected form. If you\'re aiming for C1 accuracy, stick with "were."\n\nOne thing that might help: Russian forms the subjunctive with "бы," so the idea of a separate verb form can feel foreign. In English, the subjunctive is just a ghost of the past tense system. Only "be" still visibly changes (were instead of was), which is why this one verb gets so much attention.\n\nHere\'s a quick exercise. Fill in the blanks with the correct form:\n\nIf I ___ you, I ___ (take) that job offer.  \nIf she ___ here today, she ___ (know) what to do.\n\nTry it, and I\'ll check your answers.', 'reasoning_content': 'The student is asking about "If I were you" vs "If I was you" — a classic subjunctive/second conditional question. They\'re C1 level, native Russian speaker. I should explain clearly, in English (since they wrote in English), with a natural teacher tone. I need to follow formatting rules: plain text, short paragraphs, no markdown headers, no bold/italic, one empty line between paragraphs. I can end with a short practice exercise.\n\nLet me structure:\n\n1. Acknowledge the question.\n2. Explain the subjunctive mood / unreal condition.\n3. Explain that "were" is the past subjunctive form of "be" used for hypotheticals.\n4. Note that "was" is increasingly accepted in informal speech, but "were" is the standard/correct form in formal writing and in fixed expressions.\n5. Why the past tense is used in conditionals (backshift to show distance from reality).\n6. Examples.\n7. Common mistake note for Russian speakers (since in Russian, "был" doesn\'t change this way, the subjunctive is formed with "бы").\n8. Short practice exercise.\n\nKeep it concise but thorough. C1 level means I can go deeper but not overwhelm. Avoid lists unless helpful — maybe one short list is okay but better to keep flowing paragraphs.\n\nLet me write it.'}, 'logprobs': None, 'finish_reason': 'stop'}], 'usage': {'prompt_tokens': 910, 'completion_tokens': 657, 'total_tokens': 1567, 'prompt_tokens_details': {'cached_tokens': 640}, 'completion_tokens_details': {'reasoning_tokens': 276}, 'prompt_cache_hit_tokens': 640, 'prompt_cache_miss_tokens': 270}, 'system_fingerprint': 'fp_a18b46594c_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402'}"}
