{"ts":"2026-08-01 15:35:47,204","level":"INFO","logger":"ai_service","request_id":"0e86f11a2ead","user_id":"-","msg":"Raw resulf from AI: {'id': '746c59b2-a90a-4a4f-9b9b-78ed5e999587', 'object': 'chat.completion', 'created': 1785598540, 'model': 'deepseek-v4-flash', 'choices': [{'index': 0, 'message': {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Here’s a simple way to think about it:\n\n"See" is usually not deliberate. It just means your eyes notice something. You don’t plan it.\n\n"I see a rainbow outside."\n\n"Look" is intentional. You move your eyes toward something on purpose.\n\n"Look at that sign."\n\n"Watch" is also intentional, but it means you follow something over time, especially something that moves or changes.\n\n"Watch the kids playing."\n\nCommon mistakes:\n\n"I watched TV" is correct. "I saw TV" is wrong unless you mean you saw the TV device itself.\n\n"I saw a movie yesterday" is fine because you experienced it. But "I watched a movie" is also fine. "I watched TV" is more natural than "I saw TV."\n\n"I saw you at the store" means I noticed you. "I was looking at you" sounds like I stared. "I was watching you" can sound like I was observing you for a while, which might feel a bit creepy.\n\nAnother classic mistake: "Look at this photo" not "See at this photo." But you can say "See this photo?" meaning "Do you notice it?"\n\nQuick tip: "watch" is for things that happen or move. You watch a game, a video, people walking. You look at something static, like a picture or a sign. You see things by chance.\n\nWant to try a quick exercise? Fill in the blanks: I ___ a bird outside my window. ___ at my new shoes! We ___ a documentary last night.', 'reasoning_content': 'We need to explain difference between look, see, watch. User is Russian native, C1 English. In Vocabulary Builder mode. Need answer in English primarily since user writes in English. Provide natural examples and common mistakes. Keep formatting clean, no markdown. Short paragraphs, one empty line between. No bold/italic. Use quotes for emphasis. Need to be thorough but clear. Let\'s craft.\n\nFirst, identify core meanings: see is involuntary (perceive with eyes), look is deliberate (direct your eyes at something), watch is deliberate and sustained (pay attention over time, often to movement/change).\n\nGive examples: I see the tree. Look at that! I watched a movie.\n\nCommon mistakes: "I saw TV" instead of watched TV. "Look at me" vs "see me". "I watched a bird" vs "I saw a bird" (both possible but different focus). Also "Can you see?" vs "Can you look?".\n\nAlso note usage with movies, TV: watch. With pictures: look at. With something happening by chance: see.\n\nProvide a small exercise suggestion.\n\nNeed to be friendly, tutor-like. Let\'s write.'}, 'logprobs': None, 'finish_reason': 'stop'}], 'usage': {'prompt_tokens': 889, 'completion_tokens': 552, 'total_tokens': 1441, 'prompt_tokens_details': {'cached_tokens': 640}, 'completion_tokens_details': {'reasoning_tokens': 238}, 'prompt_cache_hit_tokens': 640, 'prompt_cache_miss_tokens': 249}, 'system_fingerprint': 'fp_a18b46594c_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402'}"}
