{"ts":"2026-08-01 15:43:10,179","level":"INFO","logger":"ai_service","request_id":"e9a424011f74","user_id":"-","msg":"Raw resulf from AI: {'id': 'e14388af-700e-4430-9d0b-66e36fefa01d', 'object': 'chat.completion', 'created': 1785598979, 'model': 'deepseek-v4-flash', 'choices': [{'index': 0, 'message': {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Native speakers reduce "going to" to "gonna," "want to" to "wanna," and "have got to" to "gotta" because these are natural pronunciation patterns in fast, casual speech. They are not errors or slang exactly; they are relaxed forms that make speech flow more smoothly.\n\nShould you use them? Yes, in informal situations. If you are chatting with friends, texting a close colleague, or speaking casually, "gonna" and "wanna" sound completely normal. If you use "going to" and "want to" all the time, you can sound a bit stiff or overly careful. So they are a good tool for sounding more natural in everyday conversation.\n\nAvoid them in formal contexts. Do not use them in academic writing, business emails, presentations, job interviews, or any situation where you need to sound professional and precise. In those cases, use full forms. In writing, even in informal text messages, people often spell them out as "going to" unless they want to mimic speech.\n\nOne important detail: "gonna" only replaces "going to" when it is part of a verb phrase. You can say "I\'m gonna study tonight," but you cannot say "I\'m gonna the store." That would need "I\'m going to the store." So keep an eye on the word after it.\n\n"Gotta" is a bit different. It replaces "have got to" or "have to" when expressing obligation. "I gotta go" means "I have to go." But in formal speech you should say "I have to go" or "I need to go."\n\nTry this quick exercise: take the sentence "I am going to have to talk to him." Now write it the way you would say it to a friend. Then write it the way you would say it in a work meeting. Both are correct, just for different situations.', 'reasoning_content': 'We need respond to user. Need follow formatting. User asks why native speakers say gonna wanna gotta, should I use them, when natural/avoid. Need explain tailored C1, Russian speaker but in English. Keep plain text, short paragraphs, one empty line between. No markdown. Need natural correction? User didn\'t make mistakes likely. Need answer.\n\nWe need structure: explain contractions of going to, want to, have got to / have to. They are informal, common in speech. Pronunciation reductions, not slang. Should you use? For understanding definitely. For speaking, depends context. In casual conversation with friends, okay. In formal writing/speech, avoid. Also note "gonna" not used before "go" maybe "I\'m gonna go" is fine but "gonna go" reduplicated? Actually "gonna go" is common. Need nuance: "gonna" = "going to" only when future marker, not when "going" as movement + infinitive? Actually "I\'m gonna the store" wrong. It replaces "going to" in all senses? "I\'m going to study" -> "I\'m gonna study". "I\'m going to the store" cannot. So mention.\n\nNeed possibly explain "wanna" = want to, "gotta" = have got to / have to. "gotta" sometimes means "have a" as in "I\'ve gotta car"? No, not standard. "gotta" is "got to" as obligation. Also there is "hafta, coulda, shoulda" etc.\n\nNeed advice: use sparingly if learner; don\'t overuse in formal contexts. Also intonation/stress. Could mention "gonna" may be perceived as lazy if used in formal setting, but in casual speech it\'s normal. Since student C1, could say they should recognize and be able to use in informal conversation, but in writing or presentations avoid.\n\nLet\'s craft. Keep no lists maybe paragraphs. Use quotes for emphasis. Need maybe follow-up question at end. Something like "Try to rewrite this sentence in formal and casual ways: \'I am going to have to talk to him.\'" Maybe that is good.\n\nNeed response in English. Ensure no markdown.'}, 'logprobs': None, 'finish_reason': 'stop'}], 'usage'