student learn.\n• Ignore insignificant punctuation or stylistic issues that do not affect communication.\n\nResponse formatting (VERY IMPORTANT):\n\nYour responses will be displayed inside a mobile application.\n\nFollow these rules strictly.\n\n• Use plain text by default.\n• Keep the response visually clean and easy to read.\n• Write in short, well-structured paragraphs.\n• Separate paragraphs with exactly ONE empty line.\n• Never insert multiple blank lines.\n• Do not use Markdown headings (#, ##, ###).\n• Do not use horizontal separators such as --- or ***.\n• Do not use block quotes (>).\n• Do not use tables.\n• Do not use code blocks unless the user explicitly requests code.\n• Avoid bullet lists unless they genuinely improve readability.\n• Avoid numbered lists unless the user explicitly asks for steps.\n• Do NOT surround words with **bold**.\n• Do NOT surround words with *italic*.\n• Do NOT use decorative Markdown formatting.\n• If you need to emphasize a word or expression, use quotation marks instead.\n• Keep formatting minimal and consistent.\n• The response should look natural inside a modern mobile chat application.\n\nResponse structure:\n\nWhen correcting a sentence, use this natural structure:\n\nFirst briefly identify the mistake.\n\nThen show the corrected sentence.\n\nAfter that explain the reason naturally.\n\nThen provide one or two natural examples if they help.\n\nFinally, optionally suggest a short follow-up exercise.\n\nAvoid isolated titles such as:\n"Correction:"\n"Explanation:"\n"Why:"\n\nInstead, let the explanation flow naturally.\n\nAdditional instructions:\n\nImagine every answer will be displayed inside a clean mobile messaging application.\n\nPrioritize readability through good writing rather than through Markdown formatting.\n\nIf the student\'s question is unrelated to language learning, answer it normally.\n\nYou are currently operating in Writing Coach mode.\n\nYour goal is to help the student become a more confident and natural writer.\n\nGuidelines:\n\n• Focus on improving clarity, grammar, vocabulary, style, and natural expression.\n• Correct mistakes while preserving the student\'s intended meaning.\n• Explain important corrections when they provide educational value.\n• Suggest more natural or fluent alternatives whenever appropriate.\n• Encourage the student to rewrite or improve sentences rather than simply reading corrections.\n• Adapt feedback to the student\'s proficiency level.\n• Praise well-written parts in addition to correcting mistakes.\n• Prioritize communication and readability over perfect grammar.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Please analyse this paragraph. Correct grammar, improve style, explain every important mistake, and rewrite it in a more natural way. Yesterday I have went to my friend because he invited me for dinner. We was talking about our future plans and then suddenly he say that he want moving to Canada. I was surprising because he never told me about this before'}]"}
{"ts":"2026-08-01 15:47:39,389","level":"INFO","logger":"ai_service","request_id":"746b810d24a8","user_id":"-","msg":"Raw resulf from AI: {'id': '9878d696-9beb-4077-bee8-5a951f074932', 'object': 'chat.completion', 'created': 1785599240, 'model': 'deepseek-v4-flash', 'choices': [{'index': 0, 'message': {'role': 'assistant', 'content': '', 'reasoning_content': 'The user wants me to analyze a paragraph, correct grammar, improve style, explain important mistakes, and rewrite it more naturally. The student is C1 level, native Russian speaker, writing in English. I\'m in Writing Coach mode.\n\nLet me identify the mistakes:\n\n1. "I have went" - incorrect. "Have went" is wrong; should be "I went" (past simple) because "yesterday" indicates a specific past time. Also "have went" is the wrong participle form anyway (correct is "have gone"), but here past simple is right.\n\n2. "went to my friend" - unnatural. In English, we say "went to my friend\'s (place/house)" or "went to see my friend." "Went to my friend" sounds like you went to the person themselves.\n\n3. "We was talking" - should be "We were talking" - subject-verb agreement with plural "we."\n\n4. "he say that he want moving to Canada" - multiple issues:\n   - "say" should be "said" (past tense)\n   - "want moving" should be "wanted to move" - the verb pattern is "want to do something"\n   - Also the tense: "he said that he wanted to move" (or "he wants to move" - but since the main clause is past, backshift to "wanted" is natural; however, "he wants to move" could also work because it\'s still his current plan. For a C1 student, I should explain backshift briefly but keep it simple.)\n\n5. "I was surprising" - should be "I was surprised" - confusing the -ing (adjective describing effect) and -ed (adjective describing feeling) forms. "Surprising" means it causes surprise; "surprised" means you feel it.\n\n6. "he never told me about this before" - this is fine, though could be "he had never told me about this before" for past perfect to show it happened before the past moment. But "never told me before" is acceptable in informal English. For C1, I could mention the past perfect option.\n\nStyle improvements:\n- "went to my friend" → "went to my friend\'s place"\n- "for dinner" is fine but could be "for a dinner" or just "for dinner" (fine as is)\n- "was talking about our future plans" - fine\n- "suddenly he say" - "suddenly, he said" - comma after adverb\n- The overall paragraph is a bit choppy; can be made more natural with linking words.\n\nLet me think about the corrected version:\n\n"Yesterday I went to my friend\'s place because he invited me for dinner. We were talking about our future plans when suddenly he said that he wanted to move to Canada. I was surprised because he had never told me about this before."\n\nOr:\n"Yesterday I went to my friend\'s place because he had invited me for dinner. We were talking about our future plans when, out of the blue, he said he wanted to move to Canada. I was really surprised because he had never mentioned it before."\n\nThe "when" construction is nice: "We were talking about... when suddenly he said..." This is a natural way to express interruption.\n\nExplanations I should give:\n\n1. "I have went" → "I went" - With "yesterday," use Past Simple. Also note "went" is the past of "go," and the participle is "gone," so "have went" is doubly wrong.\n\n2. "went to my friend" → "went to my friend\'s place" - In English we go to places, not to people directly (in this context). "Go to my friend" would mean approach the person; for visiting, "go to my friend\'s" (short for friend\'s house) is natural.\n\n3. "We was" → "We were" - "was" only goes with I/he/she/it; "we" takes "were."\n\n4. "he say" → "he said" - past tense needed for narration.\n\n5. "want moving" → "wanted to move" - After "want" we use the infinitive "to + verb," not the -ing form. Also "wanted" for past.\n\n6. "I was surprising" → "I was surprised" - "surprising" describes the event/thing (e.g., "the news was surprising"); "surprised" describes the person\'s feeling ("I was surprised"). This is the -ed/-ing adjective distinction.\n\n7. "never told me about this before" → could be "had never told me" - Past Perfect shows the action happened before the moment of surprise in the past. Not mandatory but more precise.\n\nAlso, I could mention the "when suddenly" joining technique for style.\n\nShould I point out that "have went" is related to Russian interference? In Russian, you\'d say "Я вчера пошёл" (past) - not exactly. But it\'s better to focus on the rule.\n\nWhat about "my friend" - could also note "a friend of mine" but "my friend" is fine here.\n\nLet me also address the style: the original is fine but can be made more fluid. I can show an improved version.\n\nStructure per instructions: First briefly identify mistakes (I\'ll group them), show corrected sentence(s), explain reasons naturally, provide examples, suggest follow-up.\n\nI need to present corrections in a natural flow. Since there are many errors, I\'ll organize by going through the text in order, or group by type. For readability in a mobile chat, I\'ll probably go through the mistakes with clear but non-markdown formatting. I can use numbered list? The instructions say avoid numbered lists unless explicitly asked for steps. The user asked to "explain every important mistake" - a numbered list might genuinely improve readability here. The formatting guideline says "Avoid bullet lists unless they genuinely improve readability" and "Avoid numbered lists unless the user explicitly asks for steps." The user asked to explain every important mistake - so a structured list could help, but I should be careful. Maybe I\'ll write in short paragraphs, each addressing one or two mistakes. That\'s cleaner for mobile chat.\n\nLet me draft:\n\nThe user wrote: "Yesterday I have went to my friend because he invited me for dinner. We was talking about our future plans and then suddenly he say that he want moving to Canada. I was surprising because he never told me about this before"\n\nCorrected version:\n\n"Yesterday I went to my friend\'s place because he had invited me for dinner. We were talking about our future plans when suddenly he said that he wanted to move to Canada. I was surprised because he had never told me about this before."\n\nNow the explanations:\n\nMistake 1: "I have went" - two problems. First, "went" is past simple, the past participle of "go" is "gone," so "have went" is never correct. Second, because you say "yesterday," you need Past Simple anyway: "I went."\n\nMistake 2: "to my friend" - in English, when visiting someone at home, we say "to my friend\'s place/house" or "to my friend\'s." You go to a person, not to a person. Actually "go to my friend" can mean walking up to the friend. Use "friend\'s place."\n\nMistake 3: "We was talking" → "We were talking" - with "we," use "were."\n\nMistake 4: "he say that he want moving" → "he said that he wanted to move" - three things: past both verbs; after "want" use "to + infinitive" not "-ing."\n\nMistake 5: "I was surprising" → "I was surprised" - "surprising" is for things that cause the feeling, "surprised" is for the person feeling it.\n\nMistake 6: "he never told me about this before" - acceptable, but "had never told me" is more precise because it refers to a time before the shock. Past Perfect.\n\nStyle improvement: Instead of "and then suddenly," joining the two actions with "when" is more natural: "We were talking... when suddenly he said..." This shows the suddenness nicely.\n\nAlso "for dinner" - fine. "About our future plans" - fine.\n\nI should keep it concise but complete. Also, the instructions say praise well-written parts. I can note that the overall meaning is clear and the structure is logical.\n\nI\'ll write in a friendly tutor tone. Since the user wrote in English, I\'ll respond in English.\n\nFollow-up exercise: Suggest the student rewrite the paragraph themselves using the corrections, or write 3-4 sentences about a surprise news they received. Actually, a good exercise: "Now try writing 2-3 sentences about a time someone told you something surprising, using Past Simple, Past Continuous (was/were + -ing), and Past Perfect."\n\nLet me be careful with formatting: no bold, no italics, no headings, short paragraphs separated by one empty line.\n\nLength: This is a detailed analysis request, so a longer response is justified. But I should keep it readable.\n\nLet me draft the final response:\n\n---\n\nFirst of all, the meaning of your paragraph is completely clear, and the logical flow is good. The mistakes are mostly about verb forms and a couple of unnatural phrases. Here is a corrected, natural version:\n\n"Yesterday I went to my friend\'s place because he had invited me for dinner. We were talking about our'}, 'logprobs': None, 'finish_reason': 'length'}], 'usage': {'prompt_tokens': 936, 'completion_tokens': 2000, 'total_tokens': 2936, 'prompt_tokens_details': {'cached_tokens': 640}, 'completion_tokens_details': {'reasoning_tokens': 2000}, 'prompt_cache_hit_tokens': 640, 'prompt_cache_miss_tokens': 296}, 'system_fingerprint': 'fp_a18b46594c_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402'}"}
