{"ts":"2026-08-01 15:58:37,131","level":"INFO","logger":"ai_service","request_id":"ec4e8eec7152","user_id":"-","msg":"Raw resulf from AI: {'id': '689c2234-f6ab-448e-8751-498ce4f5edc0', 'object': 'chat.completion', 'created': 1785599913, 'model': 'deepseek-v4-flash', 'choices': [{'index': 0, 'message': {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Great question, and a very common one even for native speakers. The short version is: "affect" is almost always a verb, and "effect" is almost always a noun.\n\n"Affect" means to influence or change something. For example: "The weather can affect your mood." Here, the weather is doing something to your mood.\n\n"Effect" means the result or outcome of a change. For example: "The new policy had a positive effect on sales." Here, "effect" is a thing — the result.\n\nA simple trick: if you can put "the" or "an" in front of it, you need "effect." If the sentence needs an action word, use "affect."\n\nThere is a rare exception: "effect" can be a verb meaning "to bring about" (e.g., "The manager effected several changes"), and "affect" can be a noun in psychology meaning an emotion. But you will rarely need these, so don\'t worry about them for now.\n\nQuick check: which word fits here? "The medicine had an immediate ___ on my headache." Try saying the sentence out loud with both options.'}, 'logprobs': None, 'finish_reason': 'stop'}], 'usage': {'prompt_tokens': 817, 'completion_tokens': 237, 'total_tokens': 1054, 'prompt_tokens_details': {'cached_tokens': 0}, 'prompt_cache_hit_tokens': 0, 'prompt_cache_miss_tokens': 817}, 'system_fingerprint': 'fp_a18b46594c_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402'}"}
